<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baybars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1260">1260</a>–<a href="/years/1277">1277</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Baybars I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baybars, or Baybars I, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> who reigned from 1260 to 1277. As a general he shared in the victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> in 1260, before seizing the throne. His reign established the military credibility of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> by repelling Mongol incursions from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and capturing many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> strongholds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baybars.jpg" alt="Baybars" width="320" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 14th century artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baybars,_Medallion_IV,_Baptist%C3%A8re_de_Saint-Louis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1262 the khans fought the Ilkhanate of Persia over Azerbaijan and the Caucasus, a war that drew Berke into alliance with the Mamluk sultan <b>Baybars</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Mongol expansion met its limit, however, at the Battle of Ain Jalut in 1260, when the Mamluk army under Sultan Qutuz and the general <b>Baybars</b> defeated a Mongol force commanded by Kitbuqa in Galilee.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mamluk sultan <b>Baybars</b> took Caesarea and Arsuf in 1265, Safed in 1266 and Jaffa in 1268.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Baybars</b>' expedition of 1276 defeated King David, the Mamluks placed his relative Shakanda on the throne as a tributary, and for the next half century Egyptian armies repeatedly intervened in Makurian successions, installing and removing kings such as Shemamun, who was deposed and restored more than once.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The new state was made secure in 1260, when Sultan Qutuz and the Bahri Mamluk commander <b>Baybars</b> defeated the Mongols at Ain Jalut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Real power lay with ministers such as the Pervane, whose ambiguous dealings with the Mamluks ended in disaster: in 1277 the Mamluk sultan <b>Baybars</b> invaded and defeated a Mongol-Seljuk force at Elbistan, after which the Ilkhan Abaqa executed the Pervane and tightened direct Mongol rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=1268" aria-label="Damascus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5477 948 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Baybars"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5927 1218h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5927 1218h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=1268">What was here in 1268? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
